================================================================================

DYNAMIC CARBOX GENERATION KNOWLEDGE BASE

================================================================================

Files:
DLL/Mods/Carbox.h / Carbox.cpp
DLL/Mods/Randomizer.cpp
Purpose: Ensure the frontend car selection screen visually reflects the
randomized car pool. It achieves this by generating temporary,
modified texture atlases on the fly and seamlessly injecting them
into the game's loading pipeline.

DESIGN DECISIONS: PATH SELECTION

Several approaches were evaluated to achieve dynamic carbox replacements:

Direct OpenGL Interception
Intercepting OpenGL calls (such as glTexImage2D) was explored but discarded.
It operates at too low a level in the rendering pipeline, requiring complex
state tracking to identify which generic texture ID corresponds to a specific
frontend carbox.

Overriding CreateCarbox
Hooking the game's internal CreateCarbox logic was considered but ultimately
bypassed. Modifying internal game structures often introduces instability due
to dynamic memory allocations and rigid game logic expectations.

Chosen Approach: LoadTextureByName Hook
Intercepting the high-level texture loading function (LoadTextureByName)
proved the most robust. By catching the request just before the file is read
from disk, the mod can parse the requested file path, generate a temporary
replacement image if necessary, and silently substitute the file path pointer
before passing execution to the original function. The temporary file is
immediately deleted once the game completes the loading process.

EXECUTION FLOW

The dynamic carbox generation follows a strict step-by-step lifecycle to ensure
stability, memory safety, and visual accuracy.

Step 1: Data Persistence (Hook_LoadVanillaCarPool)
RVGL's internal memory structures are susceptible to being overwritten or used
as temporary buffers during level transitions. To guarantee that the randomized
car names and texture paths remain accessible when the carbox textures are
requested later, the mod creates a deep copy of the CarInfo pool into a
persistently managed array (s_carPool) immediately after the game builds the
vanilla pool.

Step 2: Texture Request Interception (Hook_LoadTextureByName)
Whenever the game attempts to load any texture, the hook intercepts the call.
Rather than relying on heavy regex or string conversions, it uses efficient
pointer arithmetic to search the last 11 characters of the requested file path
for the "carbox[1-5].bmp" pattern case-insensitively. If the texture is not a
carbox, execution is passed immediately to the original function.

Step 3: Grid Evaluation
If a valid carbox is requested, the system maps the current persistent s_carPool
state against the expected vanilla grid layout. It uses standard string
comparisons to evaluate if the randomized cars in those specific 9 slots differ
from the vanilla expectations. If the randomized grid perfectly matches the
vanilla grid for that specific page, generation is skipped to save processing
time.

Step 4: High-Resolution Atlas Generation
If differences are detected, the mod uses the STB image libraries to assemble a
new grid. Two major graphical challenges were solved during this phase:

Resolution Upscaling: To prevent extreme blurring when inserting high-
resolution custom car textures into standard 84x84 slots, the entire atlas
canvas is initialized at 1024x1024 (a power-of-two size ideal for GPUs).
Vanilla 84x84 assets are dynamically upscaled to 340x340, while custom car
textures are scaled down to 340x340. Because the game utilizes normalized UV
mapping, it scales the 1024x1024 texture back down to fit the UI accurately
without distortion.

Texture Bleeding Prevention: Initially, a 1x1 pixel black line rendering
artifact was observed. This occurred because OpenGL's bilinear filtering
pulled in transparent black background pixels around the grid cells during UI
scaling. This was resolved via edge padding (clamping) during atlas
generation, stretching the outermost pixel of each carbox source over the grid
gaps so the GPU blends identical colors instead of background pixels.

Step 5: File Redirection
The generated 1024x1024 atlas is saved to disk as a temporary BMP file
(e.g., "carbox_random_1.bmp"). The hook then dynamically modifies the path
pointer originally passed by the game, pointing it to the newly generated custom
atlas.

Step 6: Engine Loading and Cleanup
The hook passes execution to the original LoadTextureByName function, feeding
it the modified pointer. RVGL reads the custom BMP from the disk and allocates
it into GPU memory. Immediately after the original function returns, the hook
calls std::remove to delete the temporary file from the disk, ensuring no
garbage files are left behind in the user's installation.

NOTES

String Comparisons
When evaluating grid changes, the mod relies on standard C++ std::string
comparisons rather than raw pointer comparisons. This prevents false
positives that would occur if the mod compared the memory addresses of the
copied CarInfo names against the static vanilla name literals.

Memory Crash Prevention
When redirecting the texture path, the mod does not use strncpy_s to
overwrite the original path buffer. The game occasionally passes string
literals stored in read-only memory, which would trigger an OS-level Access
Violation if written to. Instead, the mod simply passes a new pointer to the
original function.